    "text": "The slope of normal at any point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>&nbsp;on the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;is given by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>. If the curve passes through the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><mo>&#183;</mo><mi>y</mi><mfenced><mi>e</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;is equal to",
